"joe_doufu" <joeclark77@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Then you have goblins, orcs, kobolds, hobgoblins, ogres, and trolls,
> all of which elicit basically the same set of ideas and adjectives
> depending on which stories you've read.  Generally they're ugly,
> stinking, pointy-toothed bad guys.

Although you are right this was true for NetHack, too. They are all
kind of related or not depending if you're looking at D&D, literature
or folklore. In LOTR "goblin" is more or less a synonym for "orc" where
as in D&D they are a different breed.

> How can we come up with half a dozen different chinese translations?

Well even if there is no D&D translation for Chinese there must be a
translation of LOTR. This would be a starting point.

> Perhaps things can be translated literally, with long names that
> explain what they are, but it might be better to instead change them to
> creatures that can be understood without reference to AD&D.  (For
> example, orcs might be "pig-men" and kobolds "dog-men".)

Of course there are a lot of things in NetHack that are specific
European. For example the multitude of different polearms reveal a
violent history. In every major European language you can easily find
names or translations for the polearms used in NetHack.

For Chinese there might be no exact translations. But the developer of
JNetHack had to solve the very same problem.

I wonder if what became of the Keystone Kops in the Japanese version.

